ctap2 — provenance note
=======================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `ctap2pin`).

Provenance
----------

ctap2 is a clean-room implementation from the FIDO Alliance "Client to
Authenticator Protocol (CTAP) 2.1" specification (proposed standard 2021-06-15
with the 2022-06-21 errata): §6.4 authenticatorGetInfo, §6.5.5
authenticatorClientPIN, §8.2 status codes and §11.2 USB HID (CTAPHID) message
and packet structure. The specification is public; its wire layouts and
algorithms are not copyrightable expression (merger doctrine; see
CONVENTIONS.md §5). No reference implementation's source (python-fido2,
libfido2, or any other) was read, ported or consulted as a design reference.

Oracle note (`src/fido2_vectors.zig`, `src/oracle_test.zig`,
`tools/gen_fido2_vectors.py`): the request bytes this module sends for every
authenticatorClientPIN subcommand and both PIN/UV protocols are asserted equal,
byte for byte, to those sent by Yubico's `python-fido2` SDK (version 2.2.1,
BSD-2-Clause) run once as a black-box test oracle. Only its public API was
driven (`fido2.ctap2.Ctap2` and `fido2.ctap2.pin.ClientPin` over a fake device
implementing the public `fido2.ctap.CtapDevice` interface); its source was not
consulted or copied, and no design was taken from it (root NOTICE §0's
black-box-oracle exemption applies; this SDK usage needs no entry there). The
captured bytes are frozen in `fido2_vectors.zig`; the test suite opens no
socket and runs no Python. The only values injected are the platform ECDH
scalar (via a monkeypatched `generate_private_key`) and the protocol-Two IVs
(via a monkeypatched `os.urandom`), so that the capture is reproducible.

The status-code table and the CTAPHID constants were transcribed from the
specification text.

`std.crypto` is used through the sibling modules `cbor` and `ctap2pin`
(all cryptography and all CBOR live there).

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.
